# feat-ztk.prf -- Gervais tiles for the terrain ZangbandTK adds.
#
# HAND-PICKED, and so hand-maintained.  Its sibling graf-ztk.prf is generated by
# scripts/tiles/borrow-tiles.py, which can match a monster to a donor by base,
# colour and depth because monsters carry those fields.  Terrain carries nothing
# a script could match on: which cell holds grass is a question about a picture.
#
# === Where these came from ===
#
# Nowhere new.  Every coordinate below is a cell that was already in
# 32x32.png and that nothing referenced.  Angband maps 1151 of the sheet's 3840
# cells; another 1239 unmapped cells hold art, and a contiguous block of it --
# sheet rows 23 to 25 -- is a wilderness terrain set: grass, earth, sand, water
# to three depths, cobbled road, trees, rock.  David Gervais drew for a variant
# audience and Angband has no wilderness, so nobody ever wired them up.  This
# game does have one, and it was rendering it in ASCII inside a tiled game.
#
# So no art was made, resized or altered to produce this file.  It is a mapping.
#
# === The lighting convention, which is measured rather than assumed ===
#
# Gervais gives each feature three tiles in consecutive columns:
#
#     column N     torch and los    a torch right beside you: the brightest
#     column N+1   lit              ambient light in a room: dimmer
#     column N+2   dark             remembered, or lit from elsewhere: dimmest
#
# Checked against the fifteen features graf-dvg.prf already maps all three ways:
# mean luminance runs torch > lit > dark in every one of them, and the three
# cells are consecutive.  The candidate families here were found by that same
# rule -- a run of three unmapped, fully-covered cells whose luminance descends
# -- and then each was rendered and looked at before being written down, because
# colour alone puts lava and bare earth in the same bucket, and did.

# --- Ground you walk on ----------------------------------------------------

# grass
feat:GRASS:torch:0x97:0xCB
feat:GRASS:los:0x97:0xCB
feat:GRASS:lit:0x97:0xCC
feat:GRASS:dark:0x97:0xCD

# bare earth
feat:DIRT:torch:0x98:0xAB
feat:DIRT:los:0x98:0xAB
feat:DIRT:lit:0x98:0xAC
feat:DIRT:dark:0x98:0xAD

# sand
feat:SAND:torch:0x97:0xC8
feat:SAND:los:0x97:0xC8
feat:SAND:lit:0x97:0xC9
feat:SAND:dark:0x97:0xCA

# mud -- the same earth as DIRT, wetter and darker, which is the distinction
# the two features draw in the world as well
feat:MUD:torch:0x98:0xA8
feat:MUD:los:0x98:0xA8
feat:MUD:lit:0x98:0xA9
feat:MUD:dark:0x98:0xAA

# road.  Cobbles rather than packed earth, so a road reads as a made thing at a
# glance and is worth following -- which is what roads are for here.
feat:ROAD:torch:0x98:0xE7
feat:ROAD:los:0x98:0xE7
feat:ROAD:lit:0x98:0xE8
feat:ROAD:dark:0x98:0xE9

# --- Things in the way -----------------------------------------------------

# tree.  A bush standing on grass, so a wood reads as trees *in* a meadow
# rather than as a wall of green.
feat:TREE:torch:0x98:0xD2
feat:TREE:los:0x98:0xD2
feat:TREE:lit:0x98:0xD3
feat:TREE:dark:0x98:0xD4

# mountainside
feat:ROCK:torch:0x98:0x93
feat:ROCK:los:0x98:0x93
feat:ROCK:lit:0x98:0x94
feat:ROCK:dark:0x98:0x95

# --- Water, at three depths --------------------------------------------------
#
# Deliberately three separate looks rather than one recoloured three ways: the
# difference between water you can wade and water you can drown in is a
# decision the player makes from the map, so it has to be visible from the map.

# shallow water -- pale, ripples, obviously a bottom under it
feat:WATER:torch:0x97:0xBF
feat:WATER:los:0x97:0xBF
feat:WATER:lit:0x97:0xC0
feat:WATER:dark:0x97:0xC1

# deep water -- strong blue, broken surface
feat:DEEP_WATER:torch:0x98:0x99
feat:DEEP_WATER:los:0x98:0x99
feat:DEEP_WATER:lit:0x98:0x9A
feat:DEEP_WATER:dark:0x98:0x9B

# open sea, where the world ends -- smooth and featureless, which is the point:
# there is nothing out there and nothing to read in it
feat:WORLD_EDGE:torch:0x98:0x9F
feat:WORLD_EDGE:los:0x98:0x9F
feat:WORLD_EDGE:lit:0x98:0xA0
feat:WORLD_EDGE:dark:0x98:0xA1

# --- Ways in ---------------------------------------------------------------

# a dungeon mouth: an opening with nothing visible inside it
feat:DUNGEON:*:0x97:0x93

# The town services take shop fronts from Gervais' own row of arches, which is
# where the seven stores already come from.  Two of that row were never claimed
# by Angband and both happen to fit: a cross for the healer, a crown for the
# magetower.  The other four services have no arch of their own yet and are not
# given a borrowed one -- two buildings sharing a picture is worse than a
# building that is honestly a letter, because the first is wrong silently.
feat:HEALER:*:0x96:0xA7
feat:MAGETOWER:*:0x96:0xA3

# A secret door is a wall until it is found, so it is drawn as one.  Angband
# leaves this unmapped -- upstream's own gap, not ours -- and an unmapped
# feature in a tiled game is an ASCII '#' among the pictures, which gives the
# secret away by looking different.
feat:SECRET:torch:0x96:0x92
feat:SECRET:los:0x96:0x92
feat:SECRET:lit:0x96:0x93
feat:SECRET:dark:0x96:0x94

# --- The unexplored grid ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Angband points FEAT_NONE at cell (0,0), which in this sheet is *fully
# transparent* -- so what a square nobody has walked on looks like is decided by
# whatever the front end leaves behind a transparent tile, and that is not the
# same everywhere.  On macOS it comes out white: a field of snow with the map
# drawn in it, which reads as a fault rather than as unexplored ground.
#
# 0x80:0x82 is the one fully opaque, pure black cell in all 3840 of them, and
# nothing else refers to it.  An opaque tile has no argument with any front end.
#
# Deliberately a *mapping* and not a new tile.  The three tilesets this game
# generates terrain into carry a black square of their own, appended to the
# sheet; Gervais gets none, because its 32x32.png is byte-identical to Angband's
# and LICENSE.md says so in those words.  Keeping that true is worth more than
# the convenience of drawing one.
feat:NONE:*:0x80:0x82
